  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "I know, think, say that...",
     "texto": [
      "After verbs such as **знать** (to know), **думать** (to think), **считать** (to consider) and **надеяться** (to hope), **что** is followed by a full clause: **Я знаю, что ты прав** («I know that you are right»), **Я думаю, что это хорошо** («I think that this is good»). There is always a comma before **что**.",
      "To introduce what someone said, use **сказать** in the past and **что**: **Он сказал, что придёт** («He said that he will come»)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Changes when reporting",
     "texto": [
      "When a quote is turned into reported speech, the speaker changes and the pronouns are adjusted: **«Я приду» → Он сказал, что придёт**. In Russian there is no automatic backshift of tense: the tense of the original quote is kept and the person changes according to who is speaking.",
      "With **надеяться** use **что** + clause: **Я надеюсь, что ты придёшь** («I hope you will come»)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
